## Why This Is Asked
Intersects BFS path counting and graph pruning under constraints; tests meticulous edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- BFS for unweighted shortest paths
- Path counting with modulo arithmetic
- Handling blocked nodes and unreachable cases
- Complexity: O(N+M)

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPaths(n, edges, blocked, s, t) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [v, u] of edges) if (!blocked[v] && !blocked[u]) g[v].push(u);
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  if (blocked[s] || blocked[t]) return { dist: -1, ways: 0 };
  dist[s] = 0; ways[s] = 1;
  const q = [s];
  while (q.length) {
    const v = q.shift();
    for (const u of g[v]) {
      if (dist[u] === Infinity) {
        dist[u] = dist[v] + 1; ways[u] = ways[v]; q.push(u);
      } else if (dist[u] === dist[v] + 1) {
        ways[u] = (ways[u] + ways[v]) % 1000000007;
      }
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], ways: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to weighted graphs and use Dijkstra with path counting?
- How to adapt for multiple sources and answering many queries efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced DP: partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums and both memoized and tabulated solutions. Demonstrates recurrence derivation, base cases, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Time: O(N^2 K); Space: O(NK) (or O(N))
- Edge-case handling for N < K

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, K){
  const N = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<N;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++) dp[i][1]=(pref[i]-pref[0])**2;
  for(let k=2;k<=K;k++){
    for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
      for(let j=k-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + (pref[i]-pref[j])**2);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][K];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply divide-and-conquer optimization if the cost function supports it?
- How would you adapt for at-most K segments or large N with memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you empirically determine the optimal threshold size?
- What techniques would you use to verify the algorithm's stability?
- How does this hybrid approach perform on partially sorted data?
- What modifications would be needed for parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing an augmented state space and applying Dijkstra with a resource constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra on expanded state space
- Edge relaxation with/without boost
- Path reconstruction from 3D predecessor structure

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithBoosts(adj, s, t, K){
  const n = adj.length; const INF = 1e15;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const parent = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(K+1).fill(null));
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  // min-heap assumed
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({d:0,u:s,k:0});
  while(!pq.empty()){
    const {d,u,k} = pq.pop();
    if(d !== dist[u][k]) continue;
    for(const {to,w} of adj[u]){
      if(dist[to][k] > d + w){
        dist[to][k] = d+w; parent[to][k] = {u,k};
        pq.push({d: dist[to][k], u:to, k});
      }
      if(k < K){
        const ww = Math.floor(w/2);
        if(dist[to][k+1] > d + ww){
          dist[to][k+1] = d+ww; parent[to][k+1] = {u,k};
          pq.push({d: dist[to][k+1], u:to, k:k+1});
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = 0; for(let kk=1; kk<=K; kk++) if(dist[t][kk] < dist[t][best]) best = kk;
  return {dist: dist[t][best], used: best, parent};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for negative weights with a limited number of boosts? 
- How would you verify correctness on large graphs with high K?

